ooRexx Plugin for IntelliJ IDEA
-------------------------------
Author: Alexander Seik
Date: 16.01.2017

Prerequisite:
- Java 8
- IntelliJ IDEA 2016 Community Edition or Ultimate

Start:
- Start IntelliJ IDEA
- Check out the source code: File -> New -> Project From Version Control -> Subversion
- Copy the content of <<Project_Root>>/ooRexxIDE.iml into <<Project_Root>>/.idea/<<Project_Name>.iml
- Go to: File -> Project Strucute -> Project. Create new project SDK: "IntelliJ Platform SDK" (Choose the root of Intellij IDEA you installed it to),
  select project language level to 8, and "Project Compiler Output" to <<Project_Root>>/out. (Replace <<Project_Root>> with the actual path.)
- Restart Intellij IDEA
- Generate the missing Java classes: Browse in IntelliJ IDEA for the files ooRexx.bnf and ooRexx.flex (located in resources/).
  Open the context menu (right click on the files) and select "Generate Parser Code" (for ooRexx.bnf) and "Run JFlex Generator"
  (for ooRexx.flex. On the first run you have to download files and put them into the <<Project_Root>>).
  This step has to be repeated on every change in ooRexx.bnf and ooRexx.flex.
- Go to menu Run -> Run... -> Edit Configurations -> Press on "+" -> Plugin: Make sure the "IntelliJ Platform SDK" is selected.
  Select the plugin as "Class path of module".
- Launch the plugin: In IntelliJ click on the "play"-button. Make sure that the IntelliJ SDK is selected as JRE.
  A new instance of IntelliJ will be started.
  There it is possible to add new files. All files with ooRexx file extensions will be considered by the plugin.
- Additional step "Export the plugin as JAR": Go to the root of the project sturcture where all sources are.
  Right click on the plugin root and choose in the context menu: "Prepare Plugin ... for Deployment".
  A Jar file will be created in the project root.


How to start plugin development:
A recommended starting point is the IntelliJ "Custom Language Support Tutorial" (see links below).
The tutorial shows multiple tools that are available for the platform and how to enable them to the custom language.

Structure:
src: The source folder contains the BNF file that defines the ooRexx grammer, a flex file that specifies tokenization rules, and Java classes.
      Java classes in org.oorexx.ide.intellij contain IDE specific classes that enrich the user experience (e.g. Syntax Highlighter).
gen:  generated Java classes like lexer or parser
resources: contains plugin meta data

Links (last access 16.01.2017):
Tutorial for language plugin development - http://www.jetbrains.org/intellij/sdk/docs/tutorials/custom_language_support_tutorial.html
GrammarKit: adds tokenizer (JFlex) and BNF support - https://github.com/JetBrains/Grammar-Kit
PsiViewer: IntelliJ IDEA plugin to inspect an abstract syntax tree (AST) - https://plugins.jetbrains.com/idea/plugin/227-psiviewer
Language plugin forum - https://intellij-support.jetbrains.com/hc/en-us/community/topics/200366979-IntelliJ-IDEA-Open-API-and-Plugin-Development


